    "created_at": "2026-10-27T10:45:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T10:45:25Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Folk Songs: Language, Storytelling and Regional Repertoires",
      "description": "An evidence-based introduction through Khorasan’s sung poetry, bardic storytelling and archives, with national coverage gaps identified."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "a-regional-starting-point",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Begin with a place and a language",
        "content": "A useful introduction to Iranian folk song begins with named performers and communities. Khorasan, in northeastern Iran, offers a particularly well-documented example. Its musical traditions include sung poetry in Persian, Khorasani Turkish, and Kurmanji Kurdish, alongside distinct Turkmen practices. These repertoires should not be flattened into a single Persian-language tradition. [s1]\n\nThe regional scholarship distinguishes narrative from lyric forms and identifies the quatrain as an important poetic form. Instruments include the dotar, a long-necked lute, but instrumentation and performance roles differ across the region. In eastern Khorasan, for example, singing and dotar playing may be undertaken by different performers. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "song-within-a-story",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "When a song belongs to a longer story",
        "content": "A bakhshi is a bard whose practice can combine narration, singing, and instrumental performance. In northern Khorasan, the dastan, or extended tale, can alternate spoken passages with sung poetry. The language of spoken narration may change with the audience. A recorded song can therefore be one part of a larger storytelling event rather than an independent composition in the modern concert sense. [s1]\n\nUNESCO’s published description of the Bakhshis of Khorasan identifies the two-stringed dotar as central to their practice and describes repertoires encompassing legendary, historical, religious, and mystical subjects. It records both family- or neighbourhood-based apprenticeship and teaching open to a wider range of students. This heritage description concerns a specific tradition; it does not describe every singer in Iran. [s2]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1",
          "s2"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "oral-and-written-learning",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Oral transmission can include written texts",
        "content": "Calling a repertoire oral does not mean that its performers never use writing. Research on Khorasan describes musicians learning poems and narratives from notebooks, manuscripts, and printed books, while acquiring melodic and rhythmic knowledge through oral teaching. It also documents the presentation of shorter songs from narratives formerly performed over longer gatherings. Transmission involves selection and adaptation as well as memory. [s1]",
        "source_ids": [
          "s1"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "archives-and-listening",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Listening with a record of the occasion",
        "content": "Harvard’s Loeb Music Library describes the Stephen Blum collection as containing field recordings from northern Iranian Khorasan, including material recorded in 1968–1969 and 1972. Its account distinguishes lyric songs from narrative performances and identifies the languages represented. Such documentation connects sound to research circumstances rather than presenting a recording as a timeless national sample. [s3]\n\nThe library also notes that the collection includes performers’ notebooks and printed verses intended for singing. These materials make the interaction between text and performance unusually tangible. The archive can guide further listening and research, although the current playback facilities and reuse permissions for individual recordings require separate checking. The library’s historical post alone does not establish present technical access. [s3]\n\nKhorasan demonstrates how much can be learned by asking who sang, in which language, for what audience, and within what kind of event. A national account must extend that approach to other regions rather than treating this documented case as representative of the whole country.",
        "source_ids": [
          "s3"
        ]
      }
    ],
